# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  .gitattributes — tell git and GitHub Linguist about Bantu (.b)
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
#  The Bantu programming language uses the .b extension. GitHub's
#  Linguist doesn't yet know about Bantu (a PR is pending), so we
#  mark .b files explicitly here. Once Linguist accepts Bantu, the
#  language bar will automatically show "Bantu" for any repo with
#  .b files. Until then, the *.b files are still attributed to the
#  custom "Bantu" name via the linguist-language attribute below.
#
#  Reference:
#    https://github.com/github-linguist/linguist
#    https://github.com/github-linguist/linguist/blob/master/docs/overrides.md

# 1) Mark every .b file as Bantu for Linguist
*.b   linguist-language=Bantu

# 2) Bantu source files use UTF-8 with LF line endings
*.b   text eol=lf working-tree-encoding=UTF-8

# 3) Make Bantu files diffable as text (they are plain UTF-8 source)
*.b   diff

# 4) The bantu-src/ tree is the vendored interpreter source (kept in
#    the repo so the Dockerfile can build Bantu from scratch). Mark
#    it as vendored so Linguist doesn't skew the language stats —
#    only OUR application code (server.b, public/*.b) counts.
bantu-src/** linguist-vendored

# 5) Standard library modules under lib/ are NOT vendored — they're
#    part of our app — so we explicitly un-vendor them.
lib/** -linguist-vendored

# 6) Shell scripts
*.sh  text eol=lf

# 7) Windows batch files
*.bat text eol=crlf

# 8) Binary assets — never diff, never merge
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ico binary
*.zip binary
*.exe binary
*.dll binary
*.so  binary
*.dylib binary

# 9) Markdown docs
*.md  text eol=lf

# 10) YAML configs
*.yml  text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf

# 11) JSON
*.json text eol=lf
